# Spring Boot / Java — banktransfer

Apply these conventions on every change to this project.

## Stack

- Spring Boot 4.0.x, Maven, Java 21+ (compatible with Java 25), base package `com.tdea.banktransfer`
- Data: Spring Data JPA + PostgreSQL (H2 only for tests)
- API docs: springdoc-openapi (Swagger UI at `/swagger-ui.html`)
- QR codes: ZXing (`core` + `javase`)
- Frontend: React + Vite + Axios in `frontend/`, served on `http://localhost:3000`

## Naming (always English)

- Classes, interfaces, methods, fields, parameters, packages and constants: **English only**
- Controllers: `*Controller` · Services: `*Service` · Repositories: `*Repository` · Exceptions: `*Exception`
- User-facing messages (exception messages, validation messages, UI text) are written in Spanish
- URL paths may be in Spanish (`/api/cuentas`, `/api/transferencias`, `/api/transacciones`)

## Documentation

- **Every public method must have an up-to-date Javadoc** (`@param`, `@return`, `@throws` when applicable)
- Update the Javadoc in the same change that modifies the method's behavior

## DTOs

- **DTOs are Java `record`s** in the `dto` package
- Validation with Jakarta Bean Validation on record components (`@NotBlank`, `@DecimalMin`, `@Digits`...), always with a Spanish `message`
- Entities never leave the service layer: map with a static factory `from(Entity)` on the response record
- Request records: `*Request` · Response records: `*Response`

## Java best practices

- Constructor injection only (no field `@Autowired`); fields `private final`
- Prefer records, `sealed` hierarchies (the business exceptions are `sealed`), pattern matching, text blocks, `var` only when the type is obvious
- Use `Optional` as a return type from repositories, never as a field or parameter
- Use `BigDecimal` for money (precision 19, scale 2); never `double`/`float`
- `List.of`, `Stream.toList()` and other immutable collections
- No business logic in controllers; controllers only validate (`@Valid`), delegate and choose the HTTP status

## Transactions and money

- Every money movement runs inside a single database transaction
- Lock both accounts with `PESSIMISTIC_WRITE`, always in the same order (by account number) to avoid deadlocks
- Rejected transfers are rolled back and then recorded in a separate transaction with status `REJECTED`
- Entities with balance carry `@Version` for optimistic locking as a second safeguard

## Errors

- Business errors extend `BusinessException` (sealed) and carry their `HttpStatus`
- `GlobalExceptionHandler` (`@RestControllerAdvice`) returns RFC 9457 `ProblemDetail`
- Status codes: 201 created · 400 invalid data · 404 account not found · 409 duplicate · 422 insufficient funds

## Configuration

- `application.properties` with `${ENV_VAR:default}` placeholders for DB URL, user, password and frontend origin
- `spring.jpa.open-in-view=false`
- CORS configured in `config/CorsConfig` for the frontend origin only

## Testing

- `@SpringBootTest` integration tests against H2 (`src/test/resources/application.properties`)
- Test every business rule: success, insufficient funds, same account, missing account

